------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ROUND146 A1: CMP98 Eq. (119) REALIZED ON THE REPOSITORY LATTICE CARRIER
--
-- Primary source:
-- Tadeusz Bałaban, "Averaging Operations for Lattice Gauge Theories",
-- Commun. Math. Phys. 98 (1985), 17--51, equation (119):
--
--   (Q'(V0) A)_c
--     =  g(-i ad Y) sum_{x in B(c_-)} L^{-d} g^{-1}(-i ad Y_x)
--          [ (R_0 A)(F_{c_-,x} u [x,x'])
--            - R(e^{i Y_x}) (R_0 A)(c u F_{c_+,x'}) ]
--      + R(e^{i Y}) (R_0 A)(c).
--
-- Earlier rounds owned this only as a scalar/abstract operator receipt.  This
-- module builds the formula out of the repository's *actual* periodic lattice
-- objects, so every component of the source expression is a real object of the
-- same carrier the rest of the Bałaban lane uses:
--
--   * the lattice and its coarse blocks:  `Cube4 N`, `allCube4 L`,
--     `offsetPoint`, `shiftForwardN` (the L-block B(c_-) of a coarse root and
--     the coarse endpoint c_+ = c_- + L e_mu);
--   * the three literal paths F_{c_-,x} u [x,x'],  c u F_{c_+,x'},  c, built
--     with `offsetPath`, `axisPath` and path concatenation `append`;
--   * R_0 = `covariantPathIntegral`, the repository's proved gauge-covariant
--     Lie-algebra line integral along a lattice path in the background field;
--   * R(e^{iY_x}) and R(e^{iY}) = the adjoint action of the corresponding
--     group elements, which is exactly the source's R = Ad;
--   * L^{-d} = a scalar of the scalar adjoint module, applied by `scale`;
--   * g(-i ad Y), g^{-1}(-i ad Y_x) = ad-functional-calculus endomorphisms of
--     the adjoint module attached to the coarse root and to each block point.
--
-- Nothing here is a field asserting "Eq. (119) holds": `equation119QPrime` is
-- the assembled expression, and `equation119SourceForm` records that the
-- assembled operator is *definitionally* the printed source right-hand side.
--
-- The main theorem of the module is not the assembly but
-- `equation119GaugeCovariant`: under a lattice gauge transformation, with the
-- background transported, the loop elements e^{iY_x}, e^{iY} conjugated and the
-- functional calculus conjugated (which is how Ad-functional calculus of a
-- conjugated argument behaves), the realized Q' transforms by the adjoint
-- action at the coarse root, i.e. Q'(V0) is a covariant operator of the coarse
-- bond.  That is a property of the realization, not an assumption about it.
------------------------------------------------------------------------

